Pinned input files for the sieve/strategies tests
=================================================

Both files below are in the format that gfm and benchfm write and that
gst reads: one line per factoring method,

    m c B1 B2 | p proba_p proba_(p+1) ... | c1 c2 c3 c4 |

(see sieve/strategies/README for the meaning of the columns). Note that
the format has no comment syntax -- a '#' line makes the reader abort --
which is why the provenance of each file is recorded here rather than in
the file itself.

They are pinned rather than produced on the fly because benchfm measures
wall-clock time, so the gfm/benchfm chain does not give the same output
twice even at a fixed -seed.

sample_methods.txt
------------------
A small assortment covering the six families gst splits its input into.
Used by test_gst_missing_family.sh, which amputates one family at a time
and checks that gst diagnoses it.

data_fm_25.txt
--------------
cado's own default cofactorization strategy, benched for fbb = 25.

This is the file that test_strategy_cado used to read from a hardcoded
/localdisk/trichard/cadoRSA155/data_fm_25, which no longer exists
anywhere. The parameters are those of the RSA-155 example in
sieve/strategies/README: lim = 16777216, so fbb = ceil(log2(lim+1)) = 25,
and lpb = 29, so nb_curves(29, 90) = 13 and the file has 3 + 13 = 16
methods -- the count that test_strategy_cado prints and expects.

The method list is the one generate_methods_cado() builds in
test_strategy_cado.cpp: PM1 315/2205, PP1-27 525/3255, ECM-M12 105/3255,
ECM-B12 315/5355, then twelve ECM-M12 curves with B1 += sqrt(B1) and B2
rounded to (2k+1)*105. It was then benched with

    benchfm -in <that list> -p -t -lb 25 -N 10000 -seed 1

Regenerating it takes about 100 seconds. The timings in the last four
columns were measured on one machine and are not reproducible elsewhere;
that is what makes this a pinned file rather than a generated one.

Beware that generate_methods_cado() is a fork of
facul_strategy_oneside::default_strategy() that has drifted: it uses
B2 = 17*B1 where the live code uses 50*B1, and it names MONTY12/BRENT12
where the live default is MONTYTWED12. This file therefore describes the
strategy cado used to have, not the one it has now.


expected/ -- the pinned outputs
===============================

test_strategies_pinned.sh and test_strategies_pinned_notime.sh compare what
the binaries produce against the references in expected/. Both scripts take
REGEN=1 to rewrite those references in place, which is how they were made
and how they should be remade after a deliberate change of behaviour; read
the diff before committing it.

Everything below is for one small instance, chosen so that the whole thing
runs in well under a second:

    lim0 = lim1 = 1024     lpb0 = lpb1 = 16     mfb0 = mfb1 = 26
    ncurves = 3            methods = sample_methods.txt

fbb = ceil(log2(lim+1)) = 11 here, so gst treats a cofactor of fewer than
2*fbb-1 = 21 bits as prime and hands it a trivial strategy. The band
[21, 26] is where the generator actually does work, and it is where the
references are taken.

  decomps.txt      gst -gdc -lim0 1024 -mfb0 r, for r in [21, 26],
                   concatenated with each section labelled by its file name
  oneside.txt      gst -gst_r -lim0 1024 -lpb0 16 -r0 r -ncurves 3
                   -in sample_methods.txt, for r in [21, 26]
  pairs.txt        gst -gst -r0 a -r1 b, for the four pairs 21_21, 21_26,
                   24_25 and 26_26 -- the two ends of the diagonal, one
                   cell off it, and one strongly asymmetric cell
  matrix.txt       the same four cells, from gst with no mode switch,
                   which computes the whole matrix in one go
  matrix.sha256    a digest over all 27*27 cells of that matrix: too large
                   to pin verbatim, but a change anywhere in it is noticed
  final_st_0.01    finalst -st <that matrix> -dist <a fixed distribution
  final_st_100     over the 16 pairs with r0, r1 in {21, 23, 24, 26}>
                   -t 0.01 and -t 100, two points of the slope selection
                   in compute_best_strategy()

The second script covers gfm and benchfm, which measure wall-clock time and
so never write the same bytes twice. Its references are compared with the
timing column stripped off, which is what the .notime suffix means:

  gfm_<family>.notime   gfm -lb 16 -ub 19 -m <family> -seed 1 over the
                        sieve region b1 in [100, 400] step 100, c in
                        [10, 40] step 10, for each of the six families.
                        That region keeps c well clear of b1: gfm aborts
                        in stage2_make_plan() when B2 = c*B1 lands within
                        30 of B1*B1.
  gfm_fch.txt           gfm -fch -fch_in sample_methods.txt: a convex hull
                        of a fixed file, so no measurement is involved
  benchfm_p.txt         benchfm -in sample_methods.txt -p -lb 10 -N 200
                        -seed 2; -p writes back the timings it was given,
                        so this one is reproducible as it stands
  benchfm_pt.notime     the same with -p -t
  benchfm_f3.txt        benchfm -in sample_methods.txt -f 3 -lb 10:
                        filtering a fixed file, again with no measurement

gfm -ch is deliberately not pinned. The convex hull ranks methods by
(probability, time), so timing noise changes which methods survive: eight
runs at one seed gave three different answers, with hulls of two different
sizes. It is smoke-checked only, and the same hull code is pinned through
-fch above.

Where these references are valid
================================

They were produced on x86-64 with gcc, and the two scripts only run where
that arithmetic is reproducible. CMake guards them; if they do not appear
in the ctest list, this is why.

  - Byte-exact pinning needs a double to be a double. The pipeline chooses
    between candidate strategies by comparing a (probability, time)
    trade-off, and ties are common -- several chains cost the same to the
    last bit, and the convex hull keeps whichever wins the comparison.
    On i386 with x87 arithmetic, which is what gcc selects for -m32 unless
    told -mfpmath=sse, intermediates keep 80 bits and those ties break the
    other way. The strategies that come out are just as good; they are not
    the same ones, and since what differs is a choice and not a digit, no
    tolerance in the comparison can absorb it. config/excess-precision.cpp
    is the test, via FLT_EVAL_METHOD.

  - The .notime references need a 64-bit unsigned long as well.
    survey_cofactoring_methods draws the sizes of its random composites
    through random_uniform(), which consumes ULONG_MAX worth of randomness
    per call, so a fixed -seed does not give the same stream on a 32-bit
    build, and the methods surveyed differ. That is a property of
    random_uniform() in utils, not of this directory.

Both are checks on the reference, not on the code: a 32-bit build computes
strategies that are just as good, and everything else in this directory
passes there.
